<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="1975 GIOS Torino Super Record with Gios Blue Columbus frame and period Campagnolo components."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/bikes/gios-super-record/hero.webp"/><title>GIOS Torino Super Record 1975 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/gios-super-record/"/><main class="atala-page"><header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">In the collection · Turin, Italy · 1975</p><h1>GIOS Torino<br/><em>Super Record</em></h1><p>A deep-blue Italian racer built with the details that made the Brooklyn team’s GIOS bicycles instantly recognisable.</p></header><figure class="quick-pass"><img src={`${images}/hero.webp`} alt="Blue 1975 GIOS Torino Super Record"/><figcaption>Built in the image of the Brooklyn team bicycles; it is not Roger De Vlaeminck’s actual race bicycle. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure><section class="atala-facts"><div><strong>1975</strong><span>Confirmed year</span></div><div><strong>Columbus SL</strong><span>Lugged steel</span></div><div><strong>Campagnolo</strong><span>Super Record / Record</span></div><div><strong>Turin</strong><span>Italian framebuilding</span></div></section><section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">GIOS and Brooklyn</p><h2>A team gave the colour a name</h2></div><div><p>Tolmino Gios founded the Turin company in 1948 after racing professionally and representing Italy at the 1936 Olympics. Its connection with Brooklyn began in 1973, linking the bright blue finish to the team’s stars and stripes.</p><p>Aldo Gios worked as team mechanic with Roger De Vlaeminck. When the Belgian won Paris–Roubaix in 1975, GIOS was already part of one of the decade’s strongest classics teams.</p><p>This bicycle is a tribute build, connecting the collection with that history without claiming a provenance it does not have.</p></div></section><section class="atala-details"><figure><img src={`${images}/headbadge.webp`} alt="GIOS head badge and chrome fork crown"/><figcaption>The badge, red pantographing and chrome crown define the front.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/cockpit.webp`} alt="Cinelli bars and GIOS-engraved stem"/><figcaption>Cinelli Campione del Mondo bars and 1A stem.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/seatpost.webp`} alt="GIOS-pantographed Campagnolo seatpost"/><figcaption>A small detail rewarding a closer look.</figcaption></figure></section><section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">Campagnolo</p><h2>Super Record — but not only Super Record</h2></div><div><p>The build includes an early Super Record rear derailleur, brakes, levers, chainset, pedals and headset. The front derailleur, shifters, hubs and seatpost are Record, while the bottom bracket is Nuovo Record.</p><p>That mixture reflects how Italian top bicycles were specified before complete groups were marketed as tightly as they later became. Record hubs meet Mavic Module E rims; Regina supplies chain and freewheel, with Cinelli and Galli contact points.</p></div></section><section class="atala-detail-pair"><figure><img src={`${images}/drivetrain.webp`} alt="Campagnolo drivetrain"/><figcaption>Early Super Record mechanics and slender steel construction.</figcaption></figure><div><p class="section-label">The frame</p><h2>Dated to 1975</h2><p>Marco Gios identified the Columbus SL frame as a 1975 Super Record. It was painted at GIOS in Turin, where the collection’s owner collected it from Marco and Aldo Gios.</p></div></section><section class="atala-era"><p class="section-label">Paris–Roubaix 1975</p><blockquote>De Vlaeminck won for the third time. <em>The blue bicycle became part of the image.</em></blockquote><div><p>On 13 April 1975 he beat Eddy Merckx and André Dierickx. It was the third of his four victories and cemented the connection between Brooklyn, De Vlaeminck and GIOS.</p></div></section><section class="koga-specs"><div><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>Lugged Columbus SL steel</dd></div><div><dt>Gears</dt><dd>Campagnolo Super Record / Record</dd></div><div><dt>Wheels</dt><dd>Record hubs, Mavic Module E rims</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>In the collection</dd></div></dl></section><section class="atala-closing"><p>The blue frame, Italian parts and personalised details all point towards the Brooklyn years while remaining a precise piece of craft in their own right.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1970-1979/`}>View the period →</a></div></section></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
